  "question": {
    "text": "At constant pressure, the heat of formation of a compound is not dependent on temperature, when",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "2visI",
      "text": "$\\Delta C_{p}=0$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "IgUps",
      "text": "$\\Delta C_{v}=0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "vAkwd",
      "text": "$\\Delta C_{p}>0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "GvSLX",
      "text": "$\\Delta C_{p}    < 0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Key Point According to Kirchhoff sequation\r\n$$\r\n\\Delta H_{(f)}=\\Delta H_{(i)}+C_{P}(\\Delta T)\r\n$$\r\nAt constant pressure\r\n$$\r\n\\Delta C_{p}=O\r\n$$\r\nTherefore, heat of formation $\\left(\\Delta H_{f}\\right)$ does not depend on temperature and value of $\\Delta C_{p}$ i.e. when $\\Delta C_{p}=0$\r\nHence, option (a) is the correct answer.",
    "image": null
